# Pipeline runtime artifacts
data/logs/

# Eval run outputs. New checkpoint runs are tracked, because until now a run's
# results file was only ever left untracked on the machine that produced it,
# plus a LangSmith push carrying case_id, case_type and scores. Neither record
# holds the retrieval config, which is the actual reason the numbers behind the
# Phase 6, prompt-v3, gpt-oss-120b and prompt-v4 rows in docs/EVALUATION.md
# cannot be reproduced. New sweeps stay local: they set their parameters
# explicitly and are rerunnable.
#
# This governs new files only, in two directions. Nine artifacts predate the
# rule and stay tracked -- four run_*.json from the prompt-v1/v2 era and five
# sweep outputs -- because an ignore rule cannot untrack what is already in the
# index. And a working copy that predates the rule may hold untracked run_*.json
# that `eval/results/` was hiding; those become visible, not lost, once this
# lands. Committing them is a judgement call, not a requirement -- none of them
# records a config either, so they add history rather than reproducibility.
#
# Note the trailing /*: with `eval/results/` git never descends into the
# directory, so a !negation below it is silently inert.
eval/results/*
!eval/results/run_*.json

